          "quote": "22.2 A Party may at any time, with the prior written consent of the Management Committee, assign, sell or transfer the whole of its rights and obligations under this Agreement. The Management Committee must not unreasonably withhold or delay its approval.",
          "rationale": "Paragraph 22 sits at (and in part above) the ASSIGN fallback and is mutual. Every Party — including us — may assign the whole of its rights and obligations to \"a successor of that Party\" (22.3(a)) and to a Parent Company, Subsidiary or Affiliate (22.3(b)-(d)), plus a one-time partial assignment to group companies (22.4), all without consent; 22.6 requires written notice to the other Parties of any exercise of 22.2-22.5; and all other assignments require the Management Committee's prior written consent, which \"must not unreasonably withhold or delay its approval\" (22.1-22.2). There is no change-of-control concept anywhere in the Agreement, so no other Party gets a termination or re-pricing right on a change of control of Teleglobe USA Inc.; the only exit is the symmetrical natural-term notice right in 18.1. Shortfalls against the preferred position are noted but not redlined: the consent gate runs to the Management Committee rather than to us individually, there is no bar on assignment to a competitor of ours, and \"successor\", \"Affiliate\" and \"Parent Company\" are undefined so a merger/asset-sale transferee rests on the ordinary meaning of \"successor\". We would not delete the consent-free affiliate carve-outs merely to align the clause literally with the narrower fallback, since that freedom benefits us.",
